            <ArticleTitle>Metabolic syndrome and gender differences in postprandial lipaemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>661-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Postprandial hyperlipidaemia may be a predictor of vascular risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">We evaluated postprandial lipaemia after an oral fat tolerance test (OFTT) in men (n=41) and women (n=21) with metabolic syndrome (MetS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Triglyceride (TG) levels were measured before and 2, 4, 6 and 8 h after the fat load.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Men showed a greater plasma TG response 8 h after the fat load (284+/-117 versus 224+/-126 mg/dl, P=0.029). Only fasting TG levels significantly predicted the TG area under the curve (AUC) and incremental AUC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Men had a more pronounced postprandial hypertriglyceridaemia and seem to have delayed TG clearance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
